Business Value:	HouseBroker supports housing decisions by providing a structured estimate rather than relying only on listing prices. The system demonstrates how predictive models can assist users in evaluating property values.
Expected benefits include:
•	Better understanding of how property features affect price
•	Reduced reliance on guesswork in price evaluation
•	Practical demonstration of regression and predictive modeling concepts
•	Real implementation experience using data-driven decision methods
The value of this project lies in demonstrating applied analytics in a realistic decision-support scenario rather than building a commercial real estate platform.

Special Issues or Constraints:	•	Prediction accuracy depends on dataset quality and feature representation
•	Historical datasets may not capture sudden market changes
•	The system provides estimation guidance, not financial or legal advice
•	The scope focuses on interpretable regression models

Company Background 
HouseBroker is a predictive analytics system implemented in Python that estimates housing prices using structured regression models. The purpose of this system is to support property evaluation decisions by calculating an estimated value from measurable housing attributes rather than relying only on listing prices.
Regarding the problem, housing markets are influenced by multiple interacting variables such as location, room count, accessibility, and neighborhood characteristics. These variables do not affect price independently. Instead, they collectively determine value through statistical relationships. Therefore, a regression-based model is appropriate because it quantifies the contribution of each feature.
This project does not attempt to introduce a new real estate platform. Instead, it focuses on implementing predictive modeling concepts in a working system using real datasets such as the Boston housing dataset and other public housing datasets. The emphasis is on applied analytics, interpretability, and measurable outputs.
